Normalize URL spelling consistently for storage, comparison, and display.
Use `url_normalize()` for normalized URLs and `url_humanize()` for readable
display URLs.

Requires **Python 3.11 or newer**. Normalization operates on URL text; it
does not fetch pages or follow redirects.

## Installation

Install the library and CLI:

```sh
python -m pip install url-normalize
```

Or install the CLI in its own environment with [uv]:

```sh
uv tool install url-normalize
```

## Quick start

```python
from url_normalize import url_normalize

print(url_normalize("HTTP://Example.COM:80/a/../b"))
# Output: http://example.com/b

print(url_normalize("bücher.de/résumé"))
# Output: https://xn--bcher-kva.de/r%C3%A9sum%C3%A9
```

From the terminal:

```sh
url-normalize "HTTP://Example.COM:80/a/../b"
# Output: http://example.com/b
```

## Normalization behavior

| Behavior | Input | Output |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Add a missing scheme | `example.com` | `https://example.com/` |
| Lowercase scheme and host; remove a default port |
`HTTP://Example.COM:80/` | `http://example.com/` |
| Resolve dot segments | `https://example.com/a/../b` |
`https://example.com/b` |
| Encode Unicode hosts and paths | `https://bücher.de/résumé` |
`https://xn--bcher-kva.de/r%C3%A9sum%C3%A9` |
| Remove trailing host dots, including Unicode equivalents |
`https://example.com。/` | `https://example.com/` |
| Preserve encoded path separators; uppercase escape hex |
`https://example.com/a%2fb` | `https://example.com/a%2Fb` |
| Preserve query order and repeated keys |
`https://example.com/?b=2&a=1&a=3` | `https://example.com/?b=2&a=1&a=3` |
| Preserve explicit empty query values | `https://example.com/?flag=&flag`
| `https://example.com/?flag=&flag` |

In query parameters, `+`, `%2B`, and `%20` remain distinct. Parameter
filtering is disabled by default. Fragments are preserved and normalized;
hashbang fragments (`#!`) are converted to the `_escaped_fragment_` query
parameter.

Normalization applies consistent spelling rules; it does not establish
whether two URLs serve the same content. Applications using normalized URLs
as persistent cache keys or identifiers should review the [changelog] when
upgrading.

## Python API

Both functions accept a URL as `str` or `None` and return `str` or `None`.
An empty string and `None` are returned unchanged. Malformed input can
raise `ValueError`, including `UnicodeError` for invalid hostnames.

The following options are keyword-only and apply to both functions:

| Option | Default | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `default_scheme` | `"https"` | Scheme to add when the URL has none. |
| `default_domain` | `None` | Domain to add to an absolute path such as
`/images/logo.png`. |
| `filter_params` | `False` | Retain only allowlisted query parameters. |
| `param_allowlist` | `None` | Override built-in rules with a list of names
or a dictionary mapping domains to lists. Requires `filter_params=True`. |

Unicode URL characters always use UTF-8 percent encoding.

